  <section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h2 id="how-h">How the Macro Calculator Works</h2>
    <p>First it estimates your basal metabolic rate with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, the most accurate common formula for adults, and multiplies it by your activity level to get maintenance calories (TDEE). It then adjusts for your goal, sets protein by body weight, fat as a share of calories, and fills the rest with carbohydrate.</p><p class="tp-note" style="font-size:16px!important"><span class="tp-kbd">BMR (men) = 10 &times; kg + 6.25 &times; cm &minus; 5 &times; age + 5</span><br><span class="tp-kbd">BMR (women) = 10 &times; kg + 6.25 &times; cm &minus; 5 &times; age &minus; 161</span></p>
  </section>

  <section id="macros" aria-labelledby="macros-h">
    <h2 id="macros-h">What Are Macros?</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table"><caption>The three macronutrients</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Macro</th><th scope="col">Calories per gram</th><th scope="col">Main roles</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Protein</th><td>4</td><td>Builds and repairs muscle, keeps you full</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Carbohydrate</th><td>4</td><td>Main fuel for intense exercise and the brain</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Fat</th><td>9</td><td>Hormones, vitamin absorption, long-lasting energy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="ratios" aria-labelledby="ratios-h">
    <h2 id="ratios-h">Macro Ratios by Goal</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/macros-by-goal-example.webp" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Bar chart of daily protein, carbs and fat for fat loss, maintenance and muscle gain for a 180 lb man"><figcaption>Example daily macros for three goals.</figcaption></figure><div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table"><caption>Typical targets</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">Calories</th><th scope="col">Protein</th><th scope="col">Fat</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Fat loss</th><td>TDEE &minus; 15&ndash;20%</td><td>1.8&ndash;2.4 g/kg</td><td>25&ndash;35%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Maintenance</th><td>TDEE</td><td>1.4&ndash;1.8 g/kg</td><td>25&ndash;35%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Muscle gain</th><td>TDEE + 5&ndash;10%</td><td>1.6&ndash;2.2 g/kg</td><td>20&ndash;30%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For protein alone, use the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a>. To see your lean mass and muscle level, try the <a href="/ffmi-calculator/">FFMI calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="track" aria-labelledby="track-h">
    <h2 id="track-h">How to Hit Your Macros</h2>
    <p>Track for a week or two with a food app to learn portion sizes, then switch to rough tracking if you prefer. Build each meal around a protein source, add vegetables and fruit for fiber (see the <a href="/fiber-calculator/">fiber calculator</a>), and fill the rest with carbs and fats to taste. Weigh yourself a few times a week and adjust calories by 100&ndash;200 if the trend is not moving after 2&ndash;3 weeks.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Macro Calculator FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do I calculate my macros?</h3></summary><p>Estimate your maintenance calories, adjust for your goal, then set protein (about 1.6–2.2 g per kg), fat (25–35% of calories) and give the remaining calories to carbs.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the best macro ratio for weight loss?</h3></summary><p>There is no single best ratio. High protein (about 2 g per kg) helps keep muscle and control hunger; the carb-to-fat split matters much less than total calories.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How accurate is the Mifflin-St Jeor equation?</h3></summary><p>It predicts resting metabolism within about 10% for most adults. Treat the result as a starting point and adjust based on your weight trend.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Should I count macros or calories?</h3></summary><p>Counting calories controls weight; counting protein as well helps body composition. Full macro counting is optional for most people.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How many carbs should I eat?</h3></summary><p>Usually whatever is left after protein and fat: commonly 40–50% of calories, more for endurance athletes and less on low-carb or keto diets.</p></details>
  </section>

